use ./utils ▪ codemsg

# every source kode accepts and turns into javascript that node cannot parse.
#
# found by six strategies, none of which knew what it was looking for:
# enumerating every token in every position, matrices of keyword against
# operand and operator against operand, the token fuzzer from fuzz.kode
# over many seeds, and mutation fuzzing - deleting spans out of the 4135
# snippets in this repo and seeing what the compiler made of the wreckage.
# ~93.000 sources in all. None of them raised an internal error; every one
# of them raised nothing at all.
#
# each line says what the compiler SHOULD say. they are all '???' because
# nobody has decided yet, which is the point of the file - it is the list.
#
# when one starts passing, move it to error.kode: that file is for messages
# that are settled, this one is for messages that are owed.
#
# the first line is the rule these were collected under, not a guarantee.
# a word that renders as an ordinary identifier is not javascript node
# cannot parse, and a good number of these never were - see 'a keyword with
# no operand' below. the harness is what matters: a case stays here until
# the compiler says something about it, or until someone decides it never
# should.

▸ error_todo

    ▸ a token on its own

        codemsg '\\' '???'
        codemsg '■' '???'
        codemsg '▪' '???'
        codemsg '▸' '???'
        codemsg '◆' '???'
        codemsg '○' '???'
        codemsg '●' '???'
        codemsg '⮐' '???'
        codemsg '->' '???'
        codemsg '=>' '???'

    ▸ a keyword with no operand

        # ten of these are settled and live in error.kode now: else catch
        # finally throw super extends when in await typeof. each is a
        # javascript reserved word or renders as one, so a bare one is
        # always broken.
        #
        # the rest are not compiler bugs and this section was wrong about
        # them. kode uses abs, clamp, copy, dbg, empty, first, from, last,
        # lpad, min, max, noon, of, rpad, trim and valid as ordinary
        # variable names - {new: new} and {delete: delete} are what
        # object.kode says they should compile to - so a word that renders
        # as a plain identifier is a name, and the compiler cannot tell a
        # forgotten call from a variable. 'abs' on its own compiles to the
        # identifier abs, which node parses and then throws on at runtime.
        # not a message the compiler owes.
        #
        # break and continue are the other two: what they are missing is a
        # loop, not an operand, and kode.kode:278 leans on a bare continue
        # standing on its own.
        #
        # do and case are not kode keywords at all. the lexer takes them
        # for names and the renderer hands node a reserved word, so they
        # belong with 'a reserved word used as a name' below.

        codemsg 'break' '???'
        codemsg 'continue' '???'

    ▸ a keyword on its own line

        codemsg 'x = 1\no f' '???'
        codemsg 'x = 1\nfrom' '???'

    ▸ a keyword in the wrong place

        codemsg 'a of' '???'
        codemsg 'a new' '???'
        codemsg 'a from' '???'
        codemsg 'a break' '???'
        codemsg 'a empty' '???'
        codemsg 'a valid' '???'
        codemsg 'a return' '???'
        codemsg 'd return' '???'
        codemsg 'm return' '???'
        codemsg 's return' '???'
        codemsg 't return' '???'
        codemsg 'x return' '???'
        codemsg 'a continue' '???'
        codemsg 'function in' '???'

    ▸ await outside async

        # none of these are bugs. top level await is javascript: a module may
        # await at its top level and node has run it that way since v14.8.
        # kode emits a module - every file it writes is parsed as one - so
        # 'await a' at the top of a kode file becomes 'await a' at the top of
        # a module and node takes it.
        #
        # these were here because the harness that found them parsed the
        # output as a function body, which is not what it is. re-checked with
        # node --check on a .mjs, all eleven parse. the same mistake had
        # already moved the reserved words below once.
        #
        # kept as the note it is. if kode ever stops emitting a module, this
        # section is the list to come back to.

        # codemsg '○ a' '???'
        # codemsg '○ -> a' '???'
        # codemsg 'await @' '???'
        # codemsg 'await 0' '???'
        # codemsg 'await a' '???'
        # codemsg 'await f' '???'
        # codemsg 'await t' '???'
        # codemsg 'x = ○ a' '???'
        # codemsg "await 's'" '???'
        # codemsg 'f await a' '???'
        # codemsg 'x = await a' '???'

    ▸ switch with an empty when

        codemsg 'switch x\n    else' '???'

    ▸ an operator the lexer does not have

        # none of these are in lexer.Patterns.op, so they lex as two or
        # three separate tokens and the renderer puts them back together
        # with the wrong spacing: 'a === b' comes out as 'a === =\nb'.
        # operator.kode lists <<= >>= >>>= &&= ||= in its precedence table
        # and the renderer knows how to emit them - the lexer just cannot
        # make them.

        codemsg 'a === b' '???'
        codemsg 'a !== b' '???'
        codemsg 'a && b' '???'
        codemsg 'a || b' '???'
        codemsg 'a <<= b' '???'
        codemsg 'a >>= b' '???'
        codemsg 'a >>>= b' '???'
        codemsg 'a &&= b' '???'
        codemsg 'a ||= b' '???'
        codemsg 'a **= b' '???'
        codemsg 'a &^ b' '???'
        codemsg 'x = a && b' '???'
        codemsg 'a === 1' '???'


    ▸ the ? family

        codemsg 'a?b' '???'

    ▸ a reserved word used as a name

        # words javascript will not have as a name in any mode at all. the
        # lexer takes them for names because it has no opinion, the renderer
        # hands them on as themselves, and node reads 'var' or 'with' or 'in'
        # and stops. every case here is the same rule about a different word,
        # so they are grouped by the word now rather than by where the fuzzer
        # happened to find them.
        #
        # one of them is in here because what comes out is wrong rather than
        # unparseable: 'log in' renders as '_k_.in(log,)'. the renderer reads
        # 'in' as a binary operator with no left operand, puts 'log' in the
        # left slot, and calls the _k_.in helper with one argument. node is
        # happy with it, which is why a syntax check does not find it








        codemsg 'new.a' '???'
        codemsg 'new()' '???'
        codemsg 'new = 1' '???'
        codemsg 'log new' '???'



        codemsg 'delete' '???'
        codemsg 'super.a' '???'

    ▸ a word reserved only in a module

        # these are the future reserved words and the strict mode ones. they
        # are ordinary identifiers in a sloppy script and nowhere else, and
        # kode emits a module - it uses import and export - so a module is
        # always strict and these always stop. node says 'Unexpected strict
        # mode reserved word' about every one.
        #
        # a rule that only rejects the words reserved everywhere would let
        # all ten of these through, so whatever answers this section needs
        # the strict mode list as well as the other one



    ▸ a global and a literal are not reserved words

        # these twenty are not bugs and the compiler is right to say nothing.
        # they came out of the same hunt as the two sections above and there
        # is no message owed for any of them, so they are written out here
        # rather than left above as tests that can never pass.
        #
        # 'null', 'true', 'false' and 'this' cannot be a name, but they are
        # values - 'x = null' and 'x = this' are exactly right, and 'null.a',
        # 'true()' and 'this.x' are ordinary javascript.
        #
        # 'NaN' and 'eval' are not reserved at all. they are globals, and a
        # module may bind a name that shadows one: 'NaN = 1' compiles to
        # 'var NaN; NaN = 1' and means what it says.
        #
        # what they have in common with the sections above is only the shape
        # of the hunt - a javascript word in a place a kode name went. kode
        # lexes all of them as plain vars, which is what they are.

        #   NaN        eval       null       this       true       false
        #   NaN.a      eval.a     null.a     this.a     true.a     false.a
        #   NaN()      eval()     null()     this()     true()     false()
        #   log NaN    NaN = 1

    ▸ postfix on something that cannot take one

        codemsg 'f()++' '???'

